DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ccaassuueewwoorrdd functions are designed to perform atomic compare-and-swap
     operation on the value in the usermode memory of the current process.

     The ccaassuueewwoorrdd routines reads the value from user memory with address
     _b_a_s_e, and compare the value read with _o_l_d_v_a_l.  If the values are equal,
     _n_e_w_v_a_l is written to the _*_b_a_s_e.  In case of ccaassuueewwoorrdd3322() and
     ccaassuueewwoorrdd(), old value is stored into the (kernel-mode) variable pointed
     by _*_o_l_d_v_a_l_p.  The userspace value must be naturally aligned.

     The callers of ccaassuuwwoorrdd() and ccaassuuwwoorrdd3322() functions cannot distinguish
     between -1 read from userspace and function failure.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ccaassuuwwoorrdd() and ccaassuuwwoorrdd3322() functions return the data fetched or -1
     on failure.  The ccaassuueewwoorrdd() and ccaassuueewwoorrdd3322() functions return 0 on suc‐
     cess, -1 on failure to access memory, and 1 when comparison or store
     failed.  The store can fail on load-linked/store-conditional architec‐
     tures.

